  "worked_solution": "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{2}{y}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4) & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,\\frac{2}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction of $∇g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)‖ & =‖⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
